      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terminologies related to tree\n------------------------------------\n\n1. Binary Tree -> It is a sprcial type of tree in which each\nnode can have at most 2 children .\n\n\n2. Root node -> Node sitting at the top most level of\ntree is called ROOT NODE .\n -> All Nodes are direct or indirect child of Root Node .\n\n3. Leaf node -> Node which doesn't have any child is called\nleaf node .\n\n4. Non-Leaf node -> Node which as atleast one child node\nis called Non-leaf node .\n\n5. Level of node -> Length of path or distance from root\nnode is called level of that node .\n\n6.  Ancestors -> Node which appear before particular node\nbut in the same path .\n\n7. Descendants -> Node which appear after particular\nnode but in the same path .\n\n8. Height -> Height of tree is defined as level of taht leaf\nnode +1 . \n\n9. Siblings -> Nodes which have same parents are called\nsiblings to each other .\n\n10. Nodes of same generation -> Nodes which are at the\nsame level .\n\n11. Full Binary Tree -> Binary Tree in which every node has\neither 0 or 2 children is called as full binary tree .\n\n12 . Complete Binary Tree -> A binary tree in which all the\nlevels except the last level are completely full and at the\nLAST LEVEL the nodes appear from LEFT TO RIGHT is\ncalled as a complete TREE .\n\n13. PERFECT BINARY TREE -> a type of binary tree in\nwhich every internal node has exactly two children and all\nleaf nodes are at the exact same level or depth.\n\n14. Binary search Tree -> is a special type of binary tree\nin which root node has a special relationship with its\nchildren .\n\n-> All the nodes special to root nodes appear towards its\nleft and those which are greater than root node appear\ntowards its right .\n\n-> Benfit is that searching an element  with lesser number\nof comparisons as compared to an array or binary tree .\nPrecisely a BST requires max comparisions equal to its\nheight which is never newer more than log2n where n is\nnumber of n nodes .",
