      "originalText": "Terminologies related to tree\n------------------------------------\n\n1. Binary Tree -> It is a sprcial type of tree in which each node can have at most 2 children .\n\n\n2. Root node -> Node sitting at the top most level of tree is called ROOT NODE .\n -> All Nodes are direct or indirect child of Root Node .\n\n3. Leaf node -> Node which doesn't have any child is called leaf node .\n\n4. Non-Leaf node -> Node which as atleast one child node is called Non-leaf node .\n\n5. Level of node -> Length of path or distance from root node is called level of that node .\n\n6.  Ancestors -> Node which appear before particular node but in the same path .\n\n7. Descendants -> Node which appear after particular node but in the same path .\n\n8. Height -> Height of tree is defined as level of taht leaf node +1 . \n\n9. Siblings -> Nodes which have same parents are called siblings to each other .\n\n10. Nodes of same generation -> Nodes which are at the same level .\n\n11. Full Binary Tree -> Binary Tree in which every node has either 0 or 2 children is called as full binary tree .\n\n12 . Complete Binary Tree -> A binary tree in which all the levels except the last level are completely full and at the LAST LEVEL the nodes appear from LEFT TO RIGHT is called as a complete TREE .\n\n13. PERFECT BINARY TREE -> a type of binary tree in which every internal node has exactly two children and all leaf nodes are at the exact same level or depth.\n\n14. Binary search Tree -> is a special type of binary tree in which root node has a special relationship with its children .\n\n-> All the nodes special to root nodes appear towards its left and those which are greater than root node appear towards its right .\n\n-> Benfit is that searching an element  with lesser number of comparisons as compared to an array or binary tree . Precisely a BST requires max comparisions equal to its height which is never newer more than log2n where n is number of n nodes .",
